{"id": 19, "ts": 1784067677.7759802, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I concur with Agent\u202f1\u2019s revised stance and keep my provisional recommendation on **Candidate\u202fB**.\u202fB\u2019s record gives us concrete evidence of the two non\u2011negotiable criteria for long\u2011haul operations: he is described as \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d supporting operational reliability, and he \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d confirming calmness under pressure. In addition, his ability to assess weather and his excellent computer skills demonstrate solid technical competence for international routes.\nCompared with the alternatives, Candidate\u202fA does show valuable traits\u2014he \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis,\u201d can \u201cmake correct decisions quickly,\u201d and \u201cunderstands complicated technology.\u201d However, we lack any explicit evidence of his reliability, and his \u201cdifficulty communicating ideas\u201d and \u201cegocentric\u201d attitude raise serious concerns for crew cooperation and professional communication, which are critical in a multi\u2011hour cockpit environment.\nCandidate\u202fD brings strong problem\u2011solving, concentration, and a serious sense of responsibility, but the notes also flag a \u201chot temper,\u201d being regarded as a \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all,\u201d and we have no direct observations of his crew\u2011cooperation or communication style. Those interpersonal risks, combined with the absence of evidence on his stress resilience, make him a weaker fit than B.\nCandidate\u202fC remains technically impressive\u2014anticipating danger, seeing complex connections, excellent spatial vision, and strong leadership\u2014but we have no direct observations of his operational reliability or stress resilience, which are essential safety pillars we cannot overlook.\nThus, weighing the solid, safety\u2011critical evidence for B against the gaps or concerns for the other candidate